/* Database teaching content is independent of the simulated execution and SVG architecture. */
const SD_STORAGE_CONTENT={
 's3-relational-databases':{
  intro:['A bookstore needs to connect books to authors without inventing a different author record every time a book appears. A relational database represents facts in relations and provides operations for combining them. The goal is both useful retrieval and enforceable integrity.','Tables are a familiar visual representation of relations, but their value is not just looking like a spreadsheet. Keys, constraints, queries, and transactions provide a precise contract for shared data used by many programs.'],
  fundamentals:['A row is a tuple of attribute values; columns describe those attributes with types. A primary key uniquely identifies a row and is not null. A foreign key constrains a reference to a candidate key in another or the same table. Relationships are expressed through values, not physical arrows stored between screens.','A join combines rows satisfying a condition. It does not automatically create or enforce a foreign key. Our book references author_id=1, and the authors table contains Leo at that key. When enforcement is removed, a nonexistent author reference can be stored even though a later join cannot find its target.'],
  working:['Propose a new book with an existing or nonexistent author identifier.','Check the declared primary and foreign-key constraints at the database boundary.','Store the row only if the selected schema permits it. Observe zero writes when the foreign-key check rejects it.','Resolve the relationship during a join. Compare a valid author match with an orphan; a left join can retain the orphan with a missing author.'],
  deep:['The relational model treats a relation as a set, while practical SQL query results can contain duplicates and SQL has null semantics. A join may multiply rows when several matches exist; it is not necessarily a one-to-one lookup. Types, uniqueness, foreign keys, and check constraints cover different invariants. A business condition involving multiple rows may require additional transaction design rather than a single check constraint.','A foreign key protects a declared relationship when database enforcement is active, including changes by callers that bypass frontend validation. Delete and update actions need explicit policy, such as restriction or cascading. This model assumes nonnull keys and one valid author and omits deferred constraints, concurrent changes, and SQL execution. Physical row layout and index maintenance are separate from the logical tables shown here.'],
  usage:['A catalog can store authors once and reference them from books; orders can reference customers and line items. This gives each fact a clear owner and prevents an accidental orphan when the appropriate constraints are declared.','Application permission checks still matter. A valid foreign key says that a referenced row exists, not that a caller may see or modify it. Relational integrity and authorization are separate boundaries.'],
  summary:['Keys identify and connect facts.','Joins retrieve relationships; constraints enforce declared integrity.','A relational schema does not automatically encode every business rule.'],
  check:['Why can a join fail to find the author of an accepted book when foreign-key enforcement is disabled?','The row was accepted without a declared relationship check. The later lookup cannot create the missing author; an inner join omits it and a left join can expose the missing match.'],
  glossary:{Relation:'A collection of tuples defined over attributes.','Foreign key':'A constraint on references to an existing candidate key.',Join:'An operation combining rows that satisfy a matching condition.'},
  controls:[['author','Referenced author','select',['existing','missing'],'existing'],['enforce','Declare foreign-key constraint','checkbox',true]],
  hook:'A relationship needs more than an arrow.',caption:'Insert a book, enforce its reference, then watch a join resolve the author.',
  presets:[['Valid reference',{author:'existing',enforce:true}],['Reject orphan',{author:'missing',enforce:true}],['Undeclared relationship',{author:'missing',enforce:false}]],
  challenge:'Remove the foreign-key constraint and insert a missing author reference. Explain why accepting a write does not guarantee a join match.',
  prediction:['Does a join automatically enforce every stored author reference?',['Yes, drawing the relationship is enough','No, a declared constraint is a separate mechanism'],1,'The join retrieves matches. Only an enforced relationship constraint prevents this modeled orphan from being stored.'],
  sources:[['PostgreSQL constraints','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-constraints.html'],['PostgreSQL table expressions and joins','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/queries-table-expressions.html']]
 },
 's3-nosql-databases':{
  intro:['Not every workload is naturally served through the same data interface. A session lookup, a nested product document, a graph traversal, and a wide time-series row have different access patterns. NoSQL names a broad collection of database approaches rather than one universal storage engine.','The lab focuses on document shape because it makes a concrete trade-off visible: related data can be embedded together or reached through a reference. The other NoSQL families still need their own explanation; a document animation is not a claim that all NoSQL data is JSON.'],
  fundamentals:['Key-value stores address a value by a key. Document stores organize structured documents. Wide-column stores organize data around partition and clustering choices. Graph databases make relationship traversal a central operation. Products can combine these capabilities, and their guarantees differ.','Embedding places related data in the same document. Referencing keeps a key to a separately owned fact. An embedded author can be returned with a book in one modeled read, while a reference needs another lookup. Either design still has a schema, whether enforced by the database, application, or both.'],
  working:['Choose an embedded document or a book containing an author reference.','Read the book and count whether the author is already present or requires a lookup.','Rename the author. Observe the difference between one canonical record and two duplicated copies.','Optionally propagate the second embedded update; compare write count and the consistency of the resulting copies.'],
  deep:['“Flexible schema” does not mean “no schema,” and NoSQL does not mean “no transactions.” MongoDB supports single-document atomic operations and multi-document transactions with documented constraints; other systems have different scopes and guarantees. Indexes, validation rules, document size limits, and distribution keys all constrain a usable design. The lab does not run a MongoDB server or simulate its transaction implementation.','Embedding can align one common read with one document but can duplicate information and create growing records. References can centralize updates but add resolution work and leave consistency obligations if no relationship constraint is enforced. Choose around cardinality, ownership, update frequency, and query patterns. A small embedded snapshot of a historic fact may intentionally remain unchanged while a live canonical value changes.'],
  usage:['A product document may embed bounded attributes frequently read together, while retaining a reference to a separately managed vendor. A session key-value workload may need expiry and direct lookup rather than rich document traversal.','Selection depends on specific query and guarantee requirements. Evaluate the actual product rather than treating a category label as proof of performance, availability, or consistency.'],
  summary:['NoSQL includes several data models.','Flexible representation still needs a managed schema.','Embedding trades relationship lookup against duplication and update coordination.'],
  check:['Does changing a database category automatically update all embedded author copies?','No. Duplicated facts still require a propagation or transaction policy. The lab leaves the second copy unchanged unless explicitly updated.'],
  glossary:{Document:'A structured record that may contain nested values.',Embedding:'Storing related data inside the same record.',Reference:'A key used to locate a separately stored fact.'},
  controls:[['model','Document shape','select',['embedded','referenced'],'embedded'],['rename','Rename author','checkbox',false],['propagate','Update every embedded copy','checkbox',false]],
  hook:'Where should related data live?',caption:'Open a document, follow a reference, and expose the cost of duplicate facts.',
  presets:[['One document read',{model:'embedded',rename:false,propagate:false}],['Follow a reference',{model:'referenced',rename:false,propagate:false}],['A stale copy',{model:'embedded',rename:true,propagate:false}]],
  challenge:'Rename a duplicated author and inspect the second book. Then compare a canonical reference.',
  prediction:['Can a document store require multiple reads for related data?',['Yes, references can require resolution','No, every relationship is embedded'],0,'Document modeling supports references too. The chosen representation determines this lookup path.'],
  sources:[['MongoDB embedding','https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/data-modeling/embedding/'],['MongoDB transactions','https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/transactions/']]
 },
 's3-data-modeling':{
  intro:['Before choosing a database layout, decide what facts exist, who owns them, and how the application uses them. Data modeling turns those requirements into entities, relationships, constraints, and access paths. A drawing without update rules is only part of a model.','A book’s current author name and the name printed on a historic invoice are different facts. Copying the former without an update policy can create stale data; preserving the latter may be intentional. Meaning and ownership come before optimization.'],
  fundamentals:['An entity is a distinguishable thing such as a book or author. Cardinality describes how many related entities can exist. Normalization separates facts to reduce dependency anomalies; denormalization deliberately duplicates or combines facts for selected access patterns.','A canonical record owns a live fact. A duplicated copy needs either synchronization or clearly defined snapshot semantics. Our two books share one live author name, so renaming only one embedded copy creates inconsistency for this requirement.'],
  working:['State the requirement: show the author’s current name for both books.','Choose either a canonical author reference or embedded copies in each book.','Apply a rename and count the records needing modification.','Read the book representation and inspect both copies. Decide whether the shape meets the stated requirement, not merely whether it saved a read.'],
  deep:['Normalization helps avoid insertion, update, and deletion anomalies when attributes depend on other facts. It does not prescribe a particular physical disk layout, and it does not mean every query must be painfully slow. Joins, indexes, and caching can support normalized data. Denormalization can be appropriate when the duplicate’s meaning, ownership, refresh process, and failure behavior are explicit.','Model bounded and unbounded relationships differently. Embedding an ever-growing comments collection can make one document a contention and size hotspot. A reference can avoid that growth while adding lookups. Schema changes need migration and compatibility plans; old and new representations may coexist. This tiny rename model omits migrations, concurrent updates, and transaction coordination, so its operation counts are illustrations rather than a database-selection score.'],
  usage:['A bookstore can keep authors canonical while storing an immutable purchased-title snapshot in order lines. These choices support different meanings: current catalog facts versus the historical terms of a purchase.','Begin with representative reads and writes, invariants, cardinalities, and expected growth. Then evaluate a physical representation. Later indexing and partitioning lessons show how access paths can change without silently changing the meaning of the data.'],
  summary:['A model includes meaning, ownership, and update rules.','Duplication can be intentional when snapshot semantics are explicit.','Read and write patterns must be considered together.'],
  check:['When is an old embedded name a correct value rather than a stale copy?','When it represents an intentional historical snapshot under the requirements. If the field promises the current author name, it needs a synchronization policy or canonical lookup.'],
  glossary:{Cardinality:'The number of entities that can participate in a relationship.',Normalization:'Organizing facts to reduce dependency anomalies.','Canonical record':'The authoritative owner of a live fact.'},
  controls:[['model','Live author representation','select',['referenced','embedded'],'referenced'],['rename','Rename live author','checkbox',true],['propagate','Propagate duplicated change','checkbox',false]],
  hook:'A faster read can buy a harder write.',caption:'Model one live fact and discover the update obligations created by copying it.',
  presets:[['Canonical owner',{model:'referenced',rename:true,propagate:false}],['Duplicate without policy',{model:'embedded',rename:true,propagate:false}],['Coordinate both copies',{model:'embedded',rename:true,propagate:true}]],
  challenge:'Count the reads saved and writes added by embedding. Explain the requirement that makes the stale copy a problem.',
  prediction:['Must an invoice snapshot change whenever the live catalog changes?',['Yes, every duplicate must be synchronized','No, a historic snapshot has a different meaning'],1,'The meaning of the fact determines the update rule. This lab models live author names, while an invoice can deliberately preserve historical values.'],
  sources:[['MongoDB relationship modeling','https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/data-modeling/schema-design-process/map-relationships/'],['PostgreSQL constraints','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-constraints.html']]
 },
 's3-indexing':{
  intro:['To find one book in a large table, checking every row can be wasteful. An index stores a separate searchable structure that leads to candidate row locations. It saves selected lookup work by maintaining extra data on writes.','A book index is a useful analogy for locating a topic, but database indexes also need concurrency, page splits, visibility checks, and maintenance. The animation opens a tiny tree so you can follow its routing rather than treating the index as a magic fast button.'],
  fundamentals:['A B+ tree organizes ordered keys through internal routing pages and leaf pages. The two-level conceptual tree here has root separator 5 and two leaves. An entry points toward a table row; an index entry is not necessarily the entire row representation.','A heap or table page holds row data. A buffer pool caches database pages in memory. Logical page visits, cache hits, and storage reads are different counts. A resident index page still requires lookup work even when no physical storage request is made.'],
  working:['Choose a target key directly in the record shelf or with the slider.','With an index enabled, compare the key with root separator 5 and visit the corresponding leaf.','Follow the located row to its table page and evaluate it. A missing key visits the tree but needs no table fetch in this model.','Disable the index and compare a four-page scan; warm the buffer pool to distinguish lookup work from storage I/O.'],
  deep:['Indexes cost space and write maintenance: inserts, deletes, and key updates can modify index pages, split them, and create logging work. A B+ tree supports ordered access, but equality, ranges, text search, and multidimensional predicates may favor different access methods. Composite-key order affects supported predicates. The tiny tree does not simulate splits, concurrent latches, or a full implementation.','An index is not always the cheapest plan. If most rows match, repeated random heap fetches can cost more than sequential reading. PostgreSQL may use bitmap or index-only paths when their conditions hold; index-only access still depends on visibility information. This experiment forces a simple index or scan to expose the mechanism, rather than imitating an optimizer or asserting that three visits beat four on real hardware.'],
  usage:['An account lookup by unique ID can benefit from a selective index, while a report reading most of a table may prefer a scan. Index design should follow actual predicates and ordering requirements.','Measure plans, cardinality, buffer behavior, and write cost. Adding an index changes an access path; it does not repair an incorrect query or replace access control.'],
  summary:['An index is a maintained access structure.','Index lookup and table visibility are separate work.','Selectivity and cache state affect whether an index helps.'],
  check:['Why can a lookup visit two index pages yet return no row?','A missing key can be routed to a leaf and found absent. The search structure still did work, but there is no matching row location to fetch.'],
  glossary:{'B+ tree':'An ordered search structure with routing pages and leaf entries.',Selectivity:'How small a fraction of data a predicate matches.','Buffer pool':'Memory used by a database to hold pages.'},
  controls:[['key','Target record key',1,9,5],['index','Use B+ tree access','checkbox',true],['warm','Pages already in buffer pool','checkbox',false]],
  hook:'Open the index. Follow the key.',caption:'Select a row and watch root, leaf, buffer, and data-page work separately.',
  presets:[['Cold index lookup',{key:5,index:true,warm:false}],['Full table scan',{key:5,index:false,warm:false}],['Warm lookup',{key:5,index:true,warm:true}],['Missing key',{key:9,index:true,warm:false}]],
  challenge:'Look up key 9. Explain the difference between pages visited and rows returned.',
  prediction:['Do cached pages remove all index lookup work?',['Yes, a cache hit means no work','No, they remove this modeled storage read'],1,'Routing and row evaluation still happen. A warm buffer saves the storage request, not all execution work.'],
  sources:[['PostgreSQL index types','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes-types.html'],['PostgreSQL index-only scans','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes-index-only-scans.html']]
 },
 's3-query-execution':{
  intro:['A declarative SQL query states a desired result, not exactly how every byte should move. The database parses it, checks its meaning, plans an access path, and executes operators. The path can change while the correct result remains the same.','The lab exposes a small plan and the work behind it. A point lookup can use an index; retrieving and ordering all rows needs a broader path. Shrinking sort memory can add temporary-storage work without changing the sorted result.'],
  fundamentals:['A plan is a tree of operators such as scans, filters, joins, and sorts. A planner estimates their costs using statistics and configuration. An executor performs the selected operators and emits rows. Estimated planner costs are not directly elapsed milliseconds.','A filter tests candidate rows, while a sort orders its input. A sort may need to see its input before returning the ordered output. If its working set exceeds available memory, it can spill to temporary storage. The row-capacity knob is a deliberately simplified stand-in for bytes and algorithm overhead.'],
  working:['Choose a point predicate or an all-rows ordered query.','Select the eligible access path and inspect visited pages, examined rows, and emitted results.','For the ordered query, feed rows into the sort; compare in-memory and spilled execution.','Return the same correctly ordered row set regardless of whether temporary storage was needed.'],
  deep:['Parsing, name resolution, rewriting, planning, and execution are distinguishable phases, although prepared statements and plan caches can reuse work. Real optimizers consider statistics, selectivity, joins, access methods, ordering, and parallelism. This lab only chooses from a forced simple index and scan; its model times are not EXPLAIN costs, and an available index does not oblige the planner to use it.','EXPLAIN shows the planned operator structure. EXPLAIN ANALYZE actually runs the statement, which matters for writes and expensive work, and reports observed behavior. Estimate errors can come from stale statistics or correlated predicates. Memory limits are often per operator, so concurrency can multiply resource use. This experiment’s single sort does not model every database memory-accounting rule.'],
  usage:['A catalog endpoint can use a selective lookup while a sorted export scans and sorts many records. The same schema supports both, but the plans and resource needs differ.','Inspect the actual plan before guessing which layer is slow. Separate rows examined, rows returned, temporary work, dependency waiting, and client response serialization. A fast scan can still feed an expensive sort.'],
  summary:['SQL describes results; plans describe execution paths.','Operator costs and result counts are different measurements.','A memory spill adds work without redefining query meaning.'],
  check:['Does a sort spill mean the query returns a different ordering?','No. A correct external sort produces the same requested ordering while using temporary storage and potentially more I/O.'],
  glossary:{Operator:'A step in an execution plan, such as a scan or sort.',Spill:'Moving working data to temporary storage when memory is insufficient.',Estimate:'A planner prediction rather than an observed measurement.'},
  controls:[['query','Query shape','select',['point','all'],'point'],['key','Target record key',1,9,5],['index','Allow index path for point lookup','checkbox',true],['memory','Sort capacity (rows)',2,8,8],['warm','Warm page buffers','checkbox',false]],
  hook:'The query is a request. The plan is the work.',caption:'Follow rows through access, filtering, sorting, and optional temporary I/O.',
  presets:[['Selective path',{query:'point',key:5,index:true,memory:8,warm:false}],['Ordered scan',{query:'all',index:true,memory:8,warm:false}],['Sort spills',{query:'all',index:true,memory:2,warm:false}]],
  challenge:'Enable the index with the all-rows sort selected. Explain why this model still scans rather than claiming every index can satisfy every ordering.',
  prediction:['Does having an id index automatically satisfy ORDER BY title?',['Yes, any index orders every column','No, the index key and query ordering differ'],1,'The modeled index is ordered by id. The title ordering needs a separate sort in this plan.'],
  sources:[['PostgreSQL EXPLAIN','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html'],['PostgreSQL query processing','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/overview.html']]
 },
 's3-transactions':{
  intro:['Transferring value requires a debit and a credit. If only the debit survives an error, the application has lost value. A transaction defines which operations succeed or fail as one atomic unit.','The important view is committed state, not just the values currently being calculated. A transaction can hold tentative changes that are discarded on rollback; separate already-committed statements cannot be undone by a later rollback of unrelated work.'],
  fundamentals:['BEGIN starts a transaction, COMMIT accepts its changes, and ROLLBACK aborts them. Atomicity means its effects are accepted together or not accepted. A transaction workspace in this diagram represents tentative versions rather than a literal scratch table every engine creates.','Our committed accounts begin at A=100 and B=50, and the transfer must preserve total 150. Isolation controls what competing work can observe; durability controls survival after acknowledgement. Those properties are related to transactions but should not be collapsed into one guarantee.'],
  working:['Begin with the committed balances and choose a transfer amount.','Debit A tentatively inside the transaction, or commit the debit separately with the transaction disabled.','Introduce a failure before the credit and observe rollback versus a partial transfer.','Without failure, credit B and commit the pair; compare workspace values with the newly committed balances.'],
  deep:['Atomicity applies to the operations participating in the transaction. Sending email or charging an external service is not automatically rolled back with a local database transaction. Keep transaction boundaries explicit and design compensation or reliable messaging for effects outside them. Long transactions can retain locks or old versions and interfere with maintenance, depending on the engine.','A transaction by itself does not guarantee the intended business invariant under every concurrent schedule. Read-modify-write operations need appropriate constraints, locking, atomic updates, or isolation. The amount slider stays within the initial balance and the example has one writer, so it intentionally omits overdraft and concurrent-transfer races. Recovery logging is illustrated on successful commit but is not a complete storage-engine implementation.'],
  usage:['A local money ledger or inventory reservation should define its atomic records and enforce its invariants. A bookstore can group an order row and its line items in one database transaction while separately coordinating remote payment.','Handle errors by explicitly aborting the appropriate unit and releasing resources. If the client loses the response after commit, transaction atomicity does not tell it whether the commit happened; Stage 2’s lifecycle experiment explains that uncertainty.'],
  summary:['Transactions define an atomic unit of participating work.','Tentative state is different from committed state.','External effects and concurrent invariants need additional design.'],
  check:['Why can the debit remain after a credit error with the transaction disabled?','The debit was committed as its own statement in this model. There is no encompassing atomic unit that can discard both operations together.'],
  glossary:{Commit:'Accepting transaction effects as committed state.',Rollback:'Aborting a transaction’s tentative effects.',Atomicity:'Accepting all participating effects together or none.'},
  controls:[['amount','Transfer amount',10,60,30],['atomic','Wrap both writes in a transaction','checkbox',true],['fail','Fail before credit','checkbox',false]],
  hook:'A debit is only half a transfer.',caption:'Watch tentative balances, committed balances, and rollback diverge.',
  presets:[['Atomic transfer',{amount:30,atomic:true,fail:false}],['Rollback error',{amount:30,atomic:true,fail:true}],['Partial write',{amount:30,atomic:false,fail:true}]],
  challenge:'Fail after the debit, then disable the transaction. Explain why the invariant changes only in the second case.',
  prediction:['Will an enclosing rollback undo a separately committed debit?',['Yes, rollback undoes any old write','No, that debit is outside the failed atomic unit'],1,'The disabled-transaction path commits statements separately. The later failure cannot retroactively merge them into one transaction.'],
  sources:[['PostgreSQL transaction tutorial','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-transactions.html'],['PostgreSQL WAL','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/wal-intro.html']]
 },
 's3-acid-properties':{
  intro:['“The database supports ACID” is useful only when each property is understood and the configuration supports the required guarantee. Atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability describe different aspects of correct transaction behavior.','A committed transfer can preserve the account total yet disappear after a crash if acknowledgement preceded durable logging. This lab separates that durability choice from the earlier all-or-nothing failure experiment.'],
  fundamentals:['Atomicity defines the accepted unit of effects. Consistency here means preserving declared invariants through valid transactions, not replica freshness. Isolation constrains interaction between concurrent work. Durability concerns survival of accepted commits under the stated failure and storage assumptions.','Write-ahead logging records recovery information before dependent data-page writes. A durable acknowledgement can wait for the required log flush while dirty data pages are written later. This diagram distinguishes an in-memory commit record from a flushed one.'],
  working:['Prepare both account updates as tentative changes in an atomic transaction.','Record the commit and optionally flush the recovery log before acknowledging it.','Introduce a crash after acknowledgement. Observe whether the model can recover the recent commit.','Check both the account invariant and whether the acknowledged result survived; they answer different questions.'],
  deep:['WAL permits recovery without forcing every changed data page to storage at each commit. Correct ordering and truthful flush behavior from the operating system and storage hardware matter. Asynchronous commit can acknowledge before the log is durable and lose a recent committed transaction after failure; the model loses that whole recent transfer rather than inventing a half-transfer crash.','ACID consistency is not a promise that arbitrary application code is correct. Constraints and transaction logic must encode the intended invariants. Isolation levels permit different anomalies, and local durability is not a backup, disaster-recovery plan, or cross-region replication guarantee. This example assumes intact storage and a simple recoverable log; it does not simulate checkpoints, corruption, or every PostgreSQL commit configuration.'],
  usage:['A financial ledger may require durable acknowledgement before confirming success to a user. A low-value telemetry workload might deliberately accept a configured window of recent data loss to reduce waiting, but that trade-off must be explicit.','Document the failure scope: process restart, machine power loss, storage loss, and regional disaster require different mechanisms. Later replication and reliability lessons extend these boundaries rather than changing the meaning of local durability.'],
  summary:['ACID properties describe different guarantees.','WAL flush and data-page flush are distinct.','An invariant can hold even when a recent acknowledged commit is lost.'],
  check:['After a crash the model restores both original balances. Was the account invariant broken? Was durability preserved?','The total remains 150, so that invariant holds. If the transfer was acknowledged and then lost, the selected policy did not preserve that acknowledgement across the modeled crash.'],
  glossary:{Durability:'Survival of accepted commits under specified failure assumptions.',WAL:'Recovery records written before dependent data-page persistence.','Dirty page':'A modified buffer page not yet written to its durable location.'},
  controls:[['amount','Transfer amount',10,60,30],['durable','Flush WAL before acknowledgement','checkbox',true],['crash','Crash after acknowledgement','checkbox',true]],
  hook:'Acknowledged is not always durable.',caption:'Follow the commit record from memory to storage, then cut the power.',
  presets:[['Durable recovery',{amount:30,durable:true,crash:true}],['Recent commit lost',{amount:30,durable:false,crash:true}],['No crash yet',{amount:30,durable:false,crash:false}]],
  challenge:'Disable the pre-acknowledgement flush and crash. Separate “total preserved” from “accepted transfer survived.”',
  prediction:['Must every changed data page be flushed before a WAL-based durable acknowledgement?',['Yes, every page must be written immediately','No, durable log records can support later replay'],1,'Write-ahead logging can make the commit recoverable before all changed table pages are flushed, under the required ordering and storage assumptions.'],
  sources:[['PostgreSQL WAL introduction','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/wal-intro.html'],['PostgreSQL asynchronous commit','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/wal-async-commit.html']]
 },
 's3-transaction-isolation':{
  intro:['Two people can use the same database while seeing data at different moments. Isolation defines which concurrent effects a transaction may observe. Without understanding that boundary, two individually sensible reads can surprise an application.','Here a reader obtains 100, another transaction commits 70, and the reader asks again. The answer depends on the snapshot rule, not merely on how fast the database runs.'],
  fundamentals:['MVCC, multiversion concurrency control, retains row versions so readers can use defined visibility snapshots. A snapshot determines which committed changes qualify as visible. An uncommitted version is not automatically visible because its bytes exist somewhere in memory.','In PostgreSQL Read Committed normally takes a new snapshot for each statement. PostgreSQL Repeatable Read uses a transaction snapshot established by its first relevant statement. This example is explicitly tied to those behaviors; identically named levels can differ between database implementations.'],
  working:['Start reader T1 and record its first selected value, 100.','Have writer T2 create a tentative new version with value 70.','Read during the uncommitted write and observe 100 under either selected level.','Commit T2 and read again in T1. Compare a new statement snapshot with the retained transaction snapshot.'],
  deep:['A nonrepeatable read occurs when the same row is observed with different committed values across reads in one transaction. Snapshot isolation can avoid that observation without guaranteeing serializability. Write skew can still violate an invariant involving several rows when concurrent transactions update different rows after observing the same snapshot. The lesson’s one-row schedule does not exercise those anomalies.','Serializable execution aims for outcomes consistent with a serial ordering, and implementations may abort conflicting transactions rather than simply block everything. Applications must handle those retryable failures carefully. Explicit locking and atomic updates can address particular invariants under weaker levels. The lab animates version visibility and does not simulate full lock management, predicate detection, or garbage collection of old versions.'],
  usage:['A report may need a stable snapshot across several queries, while a simple endpoint may want each statement to see newly committed changes. The correct choice depends on what the sequence promises to the user.','An inventory or ledger invariant needs more than a comfortable snapshot story. Analyze its concurrent read and write sets and choose constraints, locks, isolation, and retry policy appropriate to that operation.'],
  summary:['Visibility is governed by snapshots, not just storage state.','Read Committed and Repeatable Read differ in snapshot scope.','A stable snapshot is not automatically serializability.'],
  check:['Does T1 read T2’s 70 before T2 commits in either modeled level?','No. Both modeled PostgreSQL levels reject dirty reads. The difference appears after commit, when the second statement either refreshes or retains its snapshot.'],
  glossary:{MVCC:'Concurrency control using multiple record versions.',Snapshot:'A rule describing changes visible to a read.','Nonrepeatable read':'Observing a row’s value change across reads in one transaction.'},
  controls:[['level','PostgreSQL isolation level','select',['Read Committed','Repeatable Read'],'Read Committed']],
  hook:'Same row. Two moments. Which version?',caption:'Interleave a reader and a writer, then inspect each transaction’s visibility.',
  presets:[['Statement snapshots',{level:'Read Committed'}],['Transaction snapshot',{level:'Repeatable Read'}]],
  challenge:'Pause before and after T2 commits. Explain why both choices reject the tentative version but differ on the later read.',
  prediction:['Does Repeatable Read guarantee that every multi-row business invariant is serializable?',['Yes, stable reads guarantee everything','No, other concurrency anomalies still matter'],1,'A stable transaction snapshot does not automatically eliminate write skew or establish serializable execution.'],
  sources:[['PostgreSQL transaction isolation','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html'],['PostgreSQL MVCC','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/mvcc-intro.html']]
 },
 's3-replication':{
  intro:['Keeping another copy of data can support read capacity and recovery, but copying takes time and needs a protocol. A replica may be healthy yet behind the primary. A successful write to one copy does not imply every copy already shows it.','The lab changes stock from 8 to 7, then reads through a replica. Watch the acknowledgement point, replication position, and returned value separately; later catch-up does not change what a caller already saw.'],
  fundamentals:['A primary accepts the modeled write. A replica replays its change stream and tracks progress. Asynchronous replication lets acknowledgement proceed without waiting for replica replay. Synchronous replication waits for a configured condition, which can mean different things in different systems.','This lab’s synchronous choice specifically waits for apply on the selected replica, resembling a remote-apply condition rather than merely receipt. Physical and logical replication carry different forms of change. The diagram uses a conceptual ordered update stream without claiming to implement either full protocol.'],
  working:['Commit the stock change locally at the primary and advance its position.','Either replay before acknowledgement under the modeled synchronous policy or acknowledge while the asynchronous replica remains behind.','Read the replica and retain the value that was actually returned.','Let an asynchronous replica catch up later, or fail the replica and inspect the missing acknowledgement or unavailable read path.'],
  deep:['Read-after-write consistency through a replica can require routing to the primary, waiting for a position, or using a documented session guarantee. Lag is not always a fixed time: backlog, network disruption, expensive apply work, and transaction boundaries influence it. Replica read scaling helps only workloads that tolerate or manage the chosen freshness boundary.','Replication alone is not failover, consensus, or backup. Promotion needs a policy for selecting a sufficiently current node, fencing old writers, and handling uncertain outcomes. Synchronous waiting can reduce recent-copy loss under a defined failure scope while sacrificing progress when required replicas are unavailable. This experiment never promotes a node and explicitly preserves a locally committed-but-unconfirmed result during the synchronous outage.'],
  usage:['A catalog may tolerate slightly old stock displays, while checkout should consult an authoritative reservation path. A report replica can isolate analytical reads from the primary but still needs monitoring for replay progress.','Plan separately for accidental deletion, corruption, and regional loss. Replication can faithfully copy an unwanted delete; historical backups and recovery procedures serve a different purpose.'],
  summary:['Replica state can lag primary state.','Acknowledgement conditions must be stated precisely.','Replication does not automatically implement failover or backup.'],
  check:['The replica catches up after returning 8. Did that caller receive 7?','No. The later replay changes future reads. The already-returned value remains 8, which the experiment records separately.'],
  glossary:{Replica:'A copy maintained through a replication mechanism.',Replay:'Applying received changes to a replica.',Lag:'A gap between a source’s progress and a replica’s progress.'},
  controls:[['mode','Acknowledgement policy','select',['asynchronous','synchronous'],'asynchronous'],['lag','Delay replica replay until after read','checkbox',true],['delay','Replay delay (model ms)',20,200,80],['fail','Replica unreachable','checkbox',false]],
  hook:'The write succeeded. This read is older.',caption:'Follow the commit stream and separate acknowledgement from replica freshness.',
  presets:[['Lagging replica',{mode:'asynchronous',lag:true,delay:80,fail:false}],['Wait for apply',{mode:'synchronous',lag:true,delay:80,fail:false}],['Required replica down',{mode:'synchronous',lag:true,delay:80,fail:true}]],
  challenge:'Return an asynchronous stale read, then replay the update. Explain why later convergence cannot rewrite the earlier response.',
  prediction:['Does replica catch-up retroactively change a value already returned to the browser?',['Yes, convergence updates the old response','No, it affects later reads'],1,'Returned values are observations at a particular time. Later replica progress does not alter that completed response.'],
  sources:[['PostgreSQL streaming replication','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/warm-standby.html'],['PostgreSQL synchronous commit settings','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-wal.html']]
 },
 's3-partitioning':{
  intro:['A growing logical table can be divided into smaller groups while queries continue to address it as one table. Partitioning can improve lifecycle management and reduce the data touched by eligible queries. It does not automatically distribute those groups across machines.','The lab divides eight records into four nonoverlapping ranges. A key predicate can exclude unrelated groups; an all-rows request still needs every group. Watch placement and pruning rather than assuming “partitioned” always means faster.'],
  fundamentals:['A partition is a group selected by a placement rule such as a range, list, or hash. Partition pruning excludes groups that cannot satisfy a compatible predicate. Partitioning can be entirely local to one database server.','Our ranges are keys 1–2, 3–4, 5–6, and 7–8. The point key identifies one group; the all-rows request visits four. An index within each partition is a separate access structure, and replicas are additional copies rather than subdivisions of ownership.'],
  working:['Inspect the placement of each record into its key range.','Select a point key or remove the routing predicate by asking for all rows.','Use the predicate to prune impossible ranges, then access the remaining groups.','Gather matching rows and compare group count with result count.'],
  deep:['Partition design should match important predicates and data lifecycle. Date ranges can make retention maintenance easier, but a query that obscures the key through an unsupported expression may prevent useful pruning. Too many small partitions can add planning and management overhead. Uneven ranges can produce hotspots even when the number of groups looks balanced.','Constraints, uniqueness, foreign keys, index creation, and partition attachment behavior depend on the database. PostgreSQL has specific rules for partitioned-table unique constraints and pruning phases. This lab uses a fixed explicit range map and does not implement its planner or DDL. It models empty results for a key beyond the configured data range without claiming that real default partitions or insert routing behave identically.'],
  usage:['A large event table can use time partitions to manage retention and targeted date queries. A catalog keyed by a different attribute might need another access path when its most common predicates do not constrain the partition key.','Partitioning should follow measured query patterns and operational needs. If independent server capacity is needed, sharding adds a network ownership boundary; the next lesson makes that distinction visible.'],
  summary:['Partitions divide ownership within a logical dataset.','Pruning requires a compatible predicate.','Local partitioning is different from distributing shards across nodes.'],
  check:['Why does an all-rows query still contact all four partitions?','No partition can be excluded under that request. The placement rule organizes data, but does not remove required results.'],
  glossary:{Partition:'A subset of a dataset selected by a placement rule.',Pruning:'Excluding groups that cannot satisfy a predicate.','Range boundary':'A limit defining which keys belong to a partition.'},
  controls:[['key','Point key',1,9,5],['scope','Query scope','select',['point','all'],'point']],
  hook:'Split the table. Skip the irrelevant parts.',caption:'Select a key and watch the planner prune nonmatching ranges.',
  presets:[['One range',{key:5,scope:'point'}],['All partitions',{key:5,scope:'all'}],['Outside ranges',{key:9,scope:'point'}]],
  challenge:'Switch from a key predicate to all rows and explain why the same layout now needs every group.',
  prediction:['Must local table partitions live on different machines?',['Yes, partitioning always means distributed nodes','No, partitions can be local'],1,'Partitioning describes subdivision. Sharding additionally distributes ownership across database nodes.'],
  sources:[['PostgreSQL table partitioning','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-partitioning.html']]
 },
 's3-sharding':{
  intro:['One database node may eventually become a capacity or ownership boundary. Sharding distributes different portions of a dataset across database nodes. A router must know where a record belongs, and queries crossing that boundary need coordination.','The diagram shows distinct shard owners rather than identical copies. Adding another owner changes placement under the simple modulo rule, so scaling out can require moving existing data as well as routing new requests.'],
  fundamentals:['A shard owns a subset of records. A shard key drives the placement decision. Replication creates copies of an owned subset; it is a separate dimension. A scatter-gather query asks several shards and combines their results.','This model uses id modulo N for N shard owners. A point key can route to one shard; a query without a usable key visits every shard. The add-shard preview counts keys whose mapping would change under N+1, without pretending a live migration has already occurred.'],
  working:['Choose the shard count and inspect the current record distribution.','Route a point request using the modulo key, or scatter an all-rows request.','Gather results from the contacted shard owners.','Preview adding one shard and count remapped keys; compare this migration cost with the unchanged current-query placement.'],
  deep:['A poor shard key can create hotspots: one popular tenant or monotonically growing range can overload an owner even while others are idle. Hashing can distribute keys but makes range queries harder. Cross-shard joins, transactions, global uniqueness, and secondary indexes need documented mechanisms rather than assumptions that local features remain unchanged across a network.','Modulo N is intentionally a naive placement rule: changing N remaps many keys. Consistent hashing, virtual buckets, or a placement directory can reduce or control migration, but still require ownership transfer, concurrent-write handling, and routing updates. This preview does not implement rebalancing or a distributed transaction. A missing modeled key returning empty is not a claim about every shard router’s behavior.'],
  usage:['A multi-tenant service might route by tenant when most operations remain within that tenant, but a single very large tenant may require another strategy. A user-keyed application may scatter global analytics unless it builds a separate reporting path.','Choose boundaries around access patterns and measured capacity. Design replicas, backups, failover, and ownership migration separately; adding shard nodes alone does not supply them.'],
  summary:['Shards divide ownership across nodes.','A usable shard key can avoid scatter-gather.','Changing placement requires a migration protocol, not just a new count.'],
  check:['Does the add-shard preview move data before the current query runs?','No. It counts the required remappings under the proposed rule. The displayed query still uses the existing placement, making the difference between planning and executing migration explicit.'],
  glossary:{Shard:'A database-node-owned subset of records.','Shard key':'The value driving distributed placement.','Scatter-gather':'Querying multiple owners and combining their results.'},
  controls:[['key','Point key',1,9,5],['shards','Current shard owners',2,4,3],['scope','Query scope','select',['point','all'],'point'],['rebalance','Preview adding one shard','checkbox',false]],
  hook:'More machines. More ownership decisions.',caption:'Route records, scatter a query, and inspect the cost of changing placement.',
  presets:[['Targeted shard',{key:5,shards:3,scope:'point',rebalance:false}],['Scatter-gather',{key:5,shards:3,scope:'all',rebalance:false}],['Migration preview',{key:5,shards:3,scope:'point',rebalance:true}]],
  challenge:'Compare a targeted request with scatter-gather, then preview adding an owner. Explain why placement changes need coordination.',
  prediction:['Is another shard simply another copy of every record?',['Yes, shards are full replicas','No, shards own different subsets'],1,'Replicas copy data; shards divide ownership. A shard can itself have replicas, but this experiment does not model them.'],
  sources:[['MongoDB sharding overview','https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/sharding/'],['MongoDB shard keys','https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/sharding-shard-key/']]
 },
 's3-sql-versus-nosql':{
  intro:['Database choice is not a contest between two labels. Begin with required operations, invariants, growth, and failure guarantees, then compare specific products and representations. SQL is a query language associated with relational systems; NoSQL encompasses several other approaches.','The side-by-side data-shape experiment gives evidence for one design decision: a live author fact can be centralized or duplicated. It deliberately avoids claiming that either category owns one representation or is universally faster.'],
  fundamentals:['Relational systems commonly offer typed schemas, joins, constraints, and transactions. Document, key-value, wide-column, and graph products emphasize other interfaces or access patterns, with product-specific guarantees. The categories overlap in modern systems.','Normalization and embedding are modeling choices, not exclusive badges. Relational systems can store JSON and denormalize; document databases can reference related records and support transactions. Compare the same required operation, not an attractive example from one side and a bad implementation from the other.'],
  working:['State the workload: read a book with its author’s current name and rename that author consistently.','Compare a referenced canonical fact with embedded copies using identical data.','Measure modeled read and write counts and inspect stale copies after a partial update.','Decide which representation meets the requirement, then identify the additional product guarantees needed for production.'],
  deep:['Evaluate query expressiveness, integrity enforcement, transaction scope, isolation, durability configuration, indexing, distribution, operational tooling, and recovery. “NoSQL is eventually consistent” and “SQL cannot scale” are inaccurate universal rules. A distributed relational product and a single-node document store have different properties that their labels alone do not reveal.','Benchmark representative distributions, concurrency, payload sizes, and failure behavior. Operation counts in this small lab do not establish actual latency or throughput, and the two shapes are not implementations of complete competing engines. A mixed architecture can be useful, but every additional store adds synchronization, backup, and observability obligations. Choose it because a defined requirement needs it, not because the architecture diagram looks more sophisticated.'],
  usage:['An order ledger can prioritize transactions and integrity while a product view uses a document-oriented representation or projection. A simple platform may serve both needs adequately from one relational database with suitable indexing and JSON fields.','Write down why the chosen product meets each requirement and what trade-offs remain. Revisit the decision when measured workload or guarantees change, rather than migrating merely because another category sounds more scalable.'],
  summary:['Compare products and requirements, not slogans.','Data shape and database category are distinct decisions.','Additional stores add coordination and operational work.'],
  check:['Does the embedded experiment prove every document database is faster than every relational database?','No. It shows one modeled representation saving a relationship lookup while adding duplication obligations. It measures neither complete engines nor real performance.'],
  glossary:{Workload:'The operations, data distribution, and concurrency a system must handle.',Guarantee:'A documented behavior under specified conditions.',Projection:'A representation organized for a selected read pattern.'},
  controls:[['model','Representation to compare','select',['referenced','embedded'],'referenced'],['rename','Include a rename workload','checkbox',true],['propagate','Coordinate all copied names','checkbox',false]],
  hook:'Choose for the workload, not the label.',caption:'Compare one requirement across two data shapes and inspect the remaining obligations.',
  presets:[['Canonical live fact',{model:'referenced',rename:true,propagate:false}],['Read-oriented copy',{model:'embedded',rename:false,propagate:false}],['Copied update workload',{model:'embedded',rename:true,propagate:true}]],
  challenge:'Describe both the lookup benefit and update cost of the copied representation before recommending a product.',
  prediction:['Does NoSQL universally mean no transactions and eventual consistency?',['Yes, those are universal definitions','No, guarantees are product-specific'],1,'Database families and products offer different transaction and consistency mechanisms. The category label is not a complete guarantee.'],
  sources:[['MongoDB transactions','https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/transactions/'],['PostgreSQL JSON types','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-json.html']]
 },
 's3-object-storage':{
  intro:['A video or book cover is not usually best represented as millions of relational byte rows. Object storage exposes named objects with metadata and bytes, commonly through an API. It is useful for large immutable or replaceable blobs.','Uploading bytes and publishing an object are distinct events. A multipart upload can stage successful parts while the final key still has no completed object. The lab makes this boundary visible instead of showing one decorative database arrow.'],
  fundamentals:['A bucket groups objects and a key identifies an object within it. Object metadata describes properties alongside its bytes. Key prefixes can resemble folders without supplying ordinary filesystem directory or in-place write semantics.','Multipart upload splits work into numbered parts associated with an upload ID. Completing the upload assembles and publishes an object according to the service’s API. Parts can be retried independently; incomplete uploads need explicit abort or lifecycle cleanup.'],
  working:['Initiate an upload for covers/atlas.bin; observe that publication is still false.','Upload three parts and optionally interrupt the second.','Retry the failed part or leave staged data incomplete.','Complete the upload and read the final key only when all required parts are present and completion is selected.'],
  deep:['Consistency guarantees are provider-specific. Amazon S3 provides strong read-after-write consistency for completed object operations, so calling all object storage eventually consistent is misleading. That guarantee does not turn staged multipart parts into a completed object or make cross-region replication instantaneous. Authorization, versioning, encryption, and lifecycle rules add other boundaries.','Multipart clients must track part numbers and completion information and verify supported checksums. An interruption does not necessarily require resending every successful part, but abandoned parts can incur cost until cleaned up. This local model has three fixed parts and no real network, credentials, signed URLs, or service limit enforcement. It illustrates publication and retry, not a full S3 client.'],
  usage:['A learning platform can store videos and illustrations as objects while retaining searchable metadata in a database. A CDN can serve published assets closer to viewers, but authorization and cache freshness need their own design.','For user uploads, separate “bytes staged” from “asset ready.” Processing, malware checks, transcoding, and metadata publication may occur after upload completion and should have explicit states.'],
  summary:['Objects combine named bytes and metadata.','Staged upload parts are not the published final object.','Consistency and API semantics depend on the provider.'],
  check:['Three parts are staged but completion has not occurred. Is the final object ready for normal retrieval?','No in this model. The completion operation publishes the final key. The parts are upload state and need completion or cleanup.'],
  glossary:{'Object key':'The name identifying an object in a bucket.','Multipart upload':'An upload lifecycle using separately transferred numbered parts.',Publication:'Making the completed object available through its final key.'},
  controls:[['fail','Interrupt part 2','checkbox',false],['retry','Retry the interrupted part','checkbox',true],['complete','Complete and publish upload','checkbox',true]],
  hook:'Uploaded parts are not a published object.',caption:'Interrupt a transfer, retry one part, and watch the publication boundary.',
  presets:[['Publish object',{fail:false,retry:true,complete:true}],['Resume one part',{fail:true,retry:true,complete:true}],['Abandoned upload',{fail:true,retry:false,complete:true}],['Staged only',{fail:false,retry:true,complete:false}]],
  challenge:'Store every part but disable completion. Explain why successfully transferred bytes are not the same as a published asset.',
  prediction:['Must a failed multipart part force every successful part to be resent?',['Yes, every upload must restart','No, a supported multipart client can retry that part'],1,'The upload ID and numbered parts support independent retries. Completion still needs a valid final part list.'],
  sources:[['Amazon S3 multipart lifecycle','https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/mpuoverview.html'],['Amazon S3 consistency model','https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/Welcome.html#ConsistencyModel']]
 },
 's3-file-storage':{
  intro:['A path such as /notes/atlas.txt feels like one thing, but opening and writing it involves several layers. Directory entries name a file, metadata identifies it, cached pages hold active bytes, and storage holds durable blocks.','A successful buffered write can update what the application reads before those bytes survive power loss. Understanding this boundary explains why writing, closing, and flushing are not interchangeable guarantees.'],
  fundamentals:['A directory maps names to file identifiers. In a POSIX-style filesystem an inode holds file metadata and references its storage organization. A file handle refers to the opened file rather than repeatedly looking up the name on every operation.','The page cache holds file data in memory. A dirty page differs from its durable version. fsync requests persistence of the file’s modified data and required metadata under the filesystem and storage guarantees; durable directory changes can require syncing the containing directory separately.'],
  working:['Resolve the path through the directory and obtain the existing file handle.','Write new bytes into the page cache and observe the old durable bytes.','Optionally flush the file and rename its directory entry. The opened file remains the same underlying object in this scoped model.','Cut power and inspect file bytes recovered from the durable layer; distinguish that check from rename recovery.'],
  deep:['Filesystem guarantees depend on the operating system, filesystem, mount options, remote protocol, and storage hardware. A return from write does not generally mean power-loss durability, and close is not a universal replacement for fsync. The model assumes flush operations truthfully persist existing-file bytes and deliberately does not reproduce journaling or hardware failure behavior.','Renaming within the applicable filesystem can atomically change namespace visibility while an open descriptor continues to refer to the file. Durability of a create or rename can require directory synchronization in addition to file synchronization. NFS and SMB add network and caching semantics; object storage presents a different API. This lab checks existing-file byte survival and explicitly leaves directory-entry crash recovery outside its scope.'],
  usage:['A local learning tool can save a small note through a filesystem. A service publishing a configuration file may use a carefully designed temporary-write, flush, and rename procedure, with directory durability considered under its target environment.','For shared remote files, document locking, consistency, and failure behavior rather than assuming local POSIX semantics apply unchanged. Large asset workloads may instead use object APIs and explicit publication states.'],
  summary:['A filename, opened handle, cached bytes, and durable blocks are different layers.','Buffered write success is not proof of power-loss durability.','File-byte durability and directory-name durability are separate concerns.'],
  check:['The file was renamed after opening. Does its existing handle automatically point to a different file?','No in this POSIX-style model. The namespace entry changes while the handle refers to the same underlying opened file. Crash durability of the rename is a separate, unmodeled issue.'],
  glossary:{Inode:'POSIX-style filesystem metadata identifying a file.','Page cache':'Memory caching file data for reads and buffered writes.',fsync:'A request to synchronize modified file data and required metadata to storage.'},
  controls:[['sync','fsync file before crash','checkbox',true],['rename','Rename the open file','checkbox',false],['crash','Cut power after write','checkbox',true]],
  hook:'The write returned. Where are the bytes?',caption:'Open the directory, change cached data, flush, and test crash survival.',
  presets:[['Durable bytes',{sync:true,rename:false,crash:true}],['Unflushed write',{sync:false,rename:false,crash:true}],['Same open file',{sync:true,rename:true,crash:false}]],
  challenge:'Disable fsync and cut power. Compare the earlier visible cached bytes with the recovered file bytes.',
  prediction:['Does a successful buffered write always mean the changed bytes survive power loss?',['Yes, write success means durable storage','No, dirty cached bytes may not be flushed'],1,'The unflushed scenario changes memory but leaves durable storage unchanged under the model’s assumptions.'],
  sources:[['Linux virtual filesystem','https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/filesystems/vfs.html'],['Linux fsync interface','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/fsync.2.html']]
 },
 's3-database-performance':{
  intro:['A slow endpoint can be limited by storage reads, poor selectivity, sorting, lock waits, network work, or serialization. Database performance starts with identifying the work actually happening instead of turning every available knob.','The lab separates logical visits, buffer hits, physical-read requests, and selected rows. Adding modeled empty pages grows a scan’s work, while a point index path stays selective. This is an access-path illustration, not a synthetic product benchmark.'],
  fundamentals:['Latency measures time for an operation; throughput measures completed work per interval. A buffer hit avoids the modeled storage request but does not eliminate CPU work. Rows examined can exceed rows returned when a predicate filters candidates.','Selectivity affects the useful access path. Working-set residency affects I/O. Queueing and contention can dominate under concurrency, which this single-query model does not include. Its storage-latency slider changes illustrative cold-read duration rather than pretending to simulate real disk specifications.'],
  working:['Select a point predicate or all-row scan and set the modeled table-page count.','Compare a forced scan with an eligible index path. Observe logical visits and output separately.','Warm the buffer pool and identify which storage requests disappear.','Increase cold-read duration and compare completed runs without treating a fast failed or empty result as better correctness.'],
  deep:['Planner statistics help estimate cardinality, and misestimates can lead to costly plans. Indexes add maintenance and space; adding every possible index can hurt write workloads. Row width, result size, buffer residency, temporary work, and concurrency all influence observed behavior. The extra pages here are explicitly empty teaching pages, not a claim that table size can grow without data in a real workload.','Measure representative operations with plans and system observations. PostgreSQL EXPLAIN ANALYZE with buffers can distinguish actual row counts and buffer activity, but running it executes the statement and adds observation overhead. Real performance is not simply pages multiplied by one fixed delay: prefetch, parallelism, OS cache, lock waits, and storage queues matter. This model intentionally isolates a few mechanisms so their effects can be understood before broader measurement.'],
  usage:['A book lookup and a full export have different workloads. A selective index may help the lookup while the export remains scan-bound and produces a large response. Monitoring only average endpoint time can conceal those distinct paths.','Change one justified factor, compare the same correct result, and measure again. Later caching, scaling, and reliability stages extend this habit to the complete request path rather than assuming every bottleneck is inside the database.'],
  summary:['Identify actual work before optimizing.','Logical visits, buffer hits, and storage reads are different.','A small deterministic model cannot establish production throughput.'],
  check:['If every page is warm, does a full scan become a constant-time operation?','No. The model avoids storage reads but still visits and evaluates its pages. CPU work and output handling remain, even before real concurrency is considered.'],
  glossary:{Latency:'Time taken by an operation.',Throughput:'Completed operations per time interval.','Working set':'The data and structures actively needed by a workload.'},
  controls:[['query','Workload','select',['point','all'],'point'],['key','Target key',1,9,5],['index','Use index for eligible lookup','checkbox',false],['pages','Table pages (extra pages are empty)',4,12,8],['warm','Working set resident','checkbox',false],['io','Cold page read (model ms)',10,150,40]],
  hook:'Find the work before chasing the time.',caption:'Change access paths and buffer residency while watching real model counters.',
  presets:[['Growing scan',{query:'point',key:5,index:false,pages:12,warm:false,io:40}],['Selective access',{query:'point',key:5,index:true,pages:12,warm:false,io:40}],['Warm full scan',{query:'all',index:false,pages:12,warm:true,io:40}]],
  challenge:'Warm a twelve-page scan. Explain why storage reads vanish but logical visits do not.',
  prediction:['Does a warm buffer remove every cost of scanning a table?',['Yes, all work disappears','No, page traversal and evaluation remain'],1,'The buffer saves modeled physical reads. The executor still visits pages and handles candidate and result rows.'],
  sources:[['PostgreSQL EXPLAIN and buffers','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html'],['PostgreSQL performance tips','https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/performance-tips.html']]
 }
};
/* Inspector descriptions explain each architecture role rather than repeat a topic summary. */
const SD_STORAGE_ACTORS={
 app:['Application boundary','The application sends an operation and observes its returned result. It cannot infer commit, visibility, or durability merely from a moving arrow. Client-visible success and internal state transitions have distinct boundaries.'],
 db:['Storage engine coordination','The database engine coordinates the selected access or update mechanism. This actor represents several real subsystems; the adjacent data views show the specific state rather than implying one universal database implementation.'],
 index:['Ordered index pages','The miniature B+ tree routes a key through a root and one leaf toward its table location. It is a separate maintained structure, and a located entry does not automatically supply a complete visible row.'],
 buffer:['Memory and page residency','This memory layer can satisfy a resident-page request without a modeled storage read. For file writes it can hold dirty bytes that differ from durable storage. Caching changes I/O, not the meaning of the operation.'],
 disk:['Durable storage boundary','This actor represents persistent bytes under the model’s stated flush assumptions. It is distinct from application memory and does not imply protection against every corruption, storage-loss, or regional failure.'],
 books:['Book representation','Book data can contain an embedded author copy or a reference to a canonical record. Inspect the live data shelf to distinguish a saved lookup from the update obligations created by duplication.'],
 authors:['Canonical author facts','An author record owns a shared live fact when books reference it. Resolving that key adds relationship work but avoids updating the same author name in every book copy.'],
 constraints:['Declared integrity gates','Constraints run at the trusted data boundary when declared and enforced. A foreign-key check prevents the modeled missing-author reference; it does not decide whether the caller is authorized to create a book.'],
 workspace:['Tentative transaction versions','This workspace visualizes not-yet-committed account changes. An atomic rollback discards them, while a separately committed statement belongs to a different unit and cannot be undone by that rollback.'],
 wal:['Recovery and replication records','An ordered log records changes for recovery or propagation in this model. In-memory records, durable records, and replica-applied records are separate milestones with different guarantees.'],
 t1:['Reader transaction','T1 chooses visible committed versions according to the selected PostgreSQL snapshot rule. It does not read T2’s tentative value simply because that version exists in the engine.'],
 t2:['Concurrent writer','T2 creates a tentative new version and later commits it. The reader’s later observation depends on its snapshot scope, not only on this writer reaching the commit event.'],
 versions:['Visible version history','Multiple versions support a reader snapshot while another writer changes the row. This conceptual history omits cleanup, full visibility metadata, and the anomalies of broader concurrent schedules.'],
 primary:['Primary ownership','The primary commits the modeled write locally. Its acknowledgement policy may or may not wait for replica apply, and a local commit without confirmation is not automatically rolled back.'],
 replica:['Independent replay progress','The replica can be reachable yet behind the primary. It may return an older value before replaying the update; later catch-up does not rewrite the value already returned to a caller.'],
 router:['Placement and query targeting','This actor derives relevant owners from a compatible key predicate. Without that information it gathers from all groups. Placement, copies, and live migration are separate mechanisms.'],
 metadata:['Object publication metadata','This layer associates the completed key with its object after successful multipart completion. Successfully staged parts are not yet the ordinary retrievable final object.'],
 parts:['Upload staging area','Numbered parts belong to a fake upload ID and may be retried independently. Staged data must be completed or cleaned up; this model never transfers real user files.'],
 directory:['Filesystem namespace','A directory maps a filename to the underlying file identity. Renaming a directory entry does not switch an already-open handle to an unrelated file; rename crash durability is outside this model.'],
 inode:['Opened-file metadata','The file handle refers to this underlying identity and its file metadata. Cached bytes, durable storage, and its directory name remain distinguishable layers even though a path looks like one object.']
};
const SD_STORAGE_QUESTIONS={
 's3-relational-databases':['What happens when the referenced author is deleted?','The schema must define a policy. Restricting the delete preserves the reference by rejecting the change; cascading can remove dependent rows; setting a reference to null requires a nullable relationship. These choices have different application meanings. A join alone does not choose a deletion policy, and a cascade is not automatically the correct behavior for every business entity.'],
 's3-nosql-databases':['Why not embed every related record?','An unbounded relationship can create a growing document, large transfers, and concentrated write contention. Embed bounded data when its ownership and lifecycle fit the parent. Reference independently changing or large collections when appropriate, and plan the additional lookups. A document limit is product-specific; flexible structure does not remove storage or contention limits.'],
 's3-data-modeling':['Can an asynchronous projection keep copies correct?','It can maintain a deliberately lagging read model if its lag and recovery semantics meet the requirement. Events need identifiable versions, safe replay, and a way to reconcile missing or out-of-order updates. Calling a duplicate a projection does not make inconsistent updates harmless; document whether the field is a live fact, a historical snapshot, or a derived view with an explicit freshness promise.'],
 's3-indexing':['What does a write cost an indexed table?','The table row and applicable index entries both need maintenance. A key update can remove an old entry and add a new one, and full pages can require structural changes. More indexes can reduce selected reads while increasing write work, log volume, and storage. The read-only tree here deliberately exposes lookup without pretending to simulate page splits or write concurrency.'],
 's3-query-execution':['Why can an accurate plan still feel slow to a browser?','The planner is choosing among database execution paths. Client-visible time also includes obtaining a connection, waiting behind other work, serializing a large representation, and transferring it over the network. A query returning many correct rows can have an inexpensive internal access path yet expensive output handling. Correlate database observations with the whole request trace before blaming a scan.'],
 's3-transactions':['Will rollback retract an email sent in the handler?','Not unless that external effect participates in an appropriate coordination mechanism. A local database transaction cannot unsend a message accepted by an independent mail service. A reliable workflow can commit intended work with a local outbox record and publish it separately with deduplication and recovery. The important first step is stating exactly which effects share the atomic boundary.'],
 's3-acid-properties':['Is a durable commit the same as a backup?','No. A durable local commit can survive the stated local crash while the storage itself remains intact. It does not preserve historical versions against an unwanted deletion or recover data after total storage loss. Backups, retention, tested restore procedures, and regional recovery protect different failure scopes. Replicas may promptly copy the same unwanted change, so they are not interchangeable with historical recovery.'],
 's3-transaction-isolation':['Why might a stronger level ask the application to retry?','A database may detect a schedule that cannot safely complete under its selected guarantees and abort one participant. Correctness then includes a bounded retry strategy for the entire appropriate transaction, not only its last statement. External side effects and uncertain outcomes still need care. Stronger isolation does not mean arbitrary concurrent application code must always succeed without conflicts.'],
 's3-replication':['How could a session request a sufficiently recent replica read?','A system can track the writer’s replication position and choose a replica that has reached it, wait within a deadline, or route the read to the primary. Each choice has latency and availability costs. Waiting for a documented progress condition is more precise than sleeping for an arbitrary number of milliseconds, because actual lag depends on backlog and failures.'],
 's3-partitioning':['Does a useful local index replace partition pruning?','They reduce work at different levels. Pruning chooses relevant groups; an index inside a group chooses candidate row locations. A query may benefit from both, one, or neither. Partition lifecycle management also has value even when a particular query touches every group. Choose a partition key for the workload and operational boundaries rather than expecting the label alone to accelerate every access.'],
 's3-sharding':['Why is ownership migration harder than copying rows?','Writes can continue while data moves. The system needs a defined owner, a strategy for catching up intervening changes, a cutover rule, and routing updates that avoid lost or duplicated effects. Our preview merely counts remapped keys. It does not claim that changing the shard-count slider executes a safe online migration, which would require a separate protocol and failure-recovery design.'],
 's3-sql-versus-nosql':['What changes when two stores both own a user fact?','A write accepted by one store and rejected by the other creates a coordination problem. Choose a canonical owner and define how another store receives projections, or adopt an appropriate transaction or workflow protocol. Two databases may support valuable access patterns, but adding the second creates migration, consistency, recovery, and observability work. A larger architecture is not evidence that the workload needed it.'],
 's3-object-storage':['Why does versioning not authorize a download?','Versioning can retain distinct object versions, but identity and access policy still determine who may retrieve them. A published object can be private, and a successful upload does not mean every caller may read it. An application should keep publication, processing readiness, authorization, and distribution as distinct states instead of treating a key’s existence as a universal permission.'],
 's3-file-storage':['Could flushing the file leave a rename vulnerable?','Yes under environments where namespace durability needs a separate directory flush. Persisting existing-file bytes and persisting the directory entry are different operations. The exact safe procedure depends on the filesystem and platform guarantees. This animation intentionally reports byte recovery only; it never claims to prove a newly created or renamed path will survive every power-loss scenario.'],
 's3-database-performance':['Why can idle-looking CPU coexist with a slow query?','The operation may be waiting for storage, a lock, a connection lease, or another dependency. CPU utilization alone cannot identify the wait. Plans, wait events, buffer observations, queue lengths, and request traces provide different evidence. The experiment isolates access and residency, so use it to understand those mechanisms and then broaden production measurement to the actual limiting resource.']
};
for(const [id,c] of Object.entries(SD_STORAGE_CONTENT)){
 SD_LESSONS[id]={...c,objectives:[c.summary[0],c.summary[1]],experiment:c.challenge};
 const nodes={...SD_STORAGE_ACTORS};for(let j=0;j<4;j++)nodes['p'+j]=['Record group '+(j+1),'This group owns the records assigned by the displayed placement rule. A highlighted group was contacted by this query; an uncontacted group is not necessarily offline or empty. Copies and ownership are different dimensions.'];
 SD_LABS[id]={hook:c.hook,caption:c.caption,challenge:c.challenge,presets:c.presets,prediction:c.prediction,nodes,
 discoveries:[SD_STORAGE_QUESTIONS[id]]};
}
